+*   **Scaling:** Performance is independent of scene geometry or volume density, scaling only with screen resolution and pixel shader complexity.
+*   **Strengths:** Decouples aesthetic post-processing from the volume rendering algorithm; allows for complex effects like bloom and vignette without modifying the ray caster.
+*   **Limitations:** Cannot affect geometry that is not visible on screen (e.g., occluded volumes); requires the scene to be rendered to a texture first.
+*   **When to use:** When applying global aesthetic effects (bloom, vignette, color grading) to the final image.
+*   **When not to use:** When effects need to interact with depth or geometry in a way that screen-space cannot represent (e.g., volumetric fog that occludes geometry).
 
 ## Background
-The specific implementation described here is part of the Minerust project, a voxel-based game engine [S1, unofficial]. The technique is also used in other contexts, such as volumetric light scattering in Fallout 4, where bilateral upsampling and dual-source blending are employed to enhance volumetric lighting results [S2, unofficial]. Frustum raycasting and blending is used in volumetric cloud rendering to integrate cloud volumes with the scene [S3, unofficial].
+Screen-space post-processing is a standard technique in modern real-time rendering, often implemented as a final pass in the rendering pipeline. The specific implementation described here is part of the "Minerust" project, a voxel-based game engine using wgpu [S1, unofficial]. The technique is widely used in games and applications to enhance visual fidelity, with variations like bloom and tone mapping being staples of HDR rendering pipelines [S2, S3, unofficial].
 
